#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167781} :activity/id #uuid "b36ed83a-cc37-5ff2-a68e-2a0e28c0b24e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048270 :topic/id #uuid "8e43115f-4416-5ec0-b698-3e53a959567b" :topic/math-academy-id 3286} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167713 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166847 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166697 :question/id #uuid "699d9c0c-bb98-5710-8d98-10b6f35ab202" :question/math-academy-id "e-8911"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77603-c824-5160-80fc-3d10353fe243" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049605}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487380}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability Using Bayes' Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "7e6025e7-7c05-542d-a78a-f9d7e627be29" :step/math-academy-id 33287 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167772}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167714 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166848 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166698 :question/id #uuid "284f5029-19eb-53d4-a3b7-0f66d33b9cc9" :question/math-academy-id "e-8912"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a4fee2d-88c3-5381-bd55-98990abc7df3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493967}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Conditional Probability"} :step/id #uuid "fd5da403-f379-5dee-9a50-ecafcf083c36" :step/math-academy-id 33288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167772 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166877 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166482 :question/id #uuid "bc694d40-d150-55e6-981f-0ca6b5d91e21" :question/math-academy-id "e-10418"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c467adb-9d0e-5b33-b81e-4959e2703535" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493903}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying Bayes’ Theorem to a Situation in Context"} :step/id #uuid "7a97606a-98ac-50e6-9e3c-78ae18820b2e" :step/math-academy-id 39256 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167714}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167781 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168143 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will generalize Bayes' theorem to deal with situations where the sample space $\\mathcal S$ is partitioned into a disjoint union of multiple events. \n\nSuppose we have three events $A_1,A_2,$ and $A_3$ that form a disjoint union of the sample space $\\mathcal S,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/89/89a78ef526fcd29a59bacd4d6ae1a456a596d6616af7453f1ba530a610d9a2ac.png)\n\n \n\nNow consider an event $B$ that also lies in the sample space:\n\n![](images/da/daa96423e45f9f80897e2c2d61177eab2faff77f440921866d1b81b7ef2cfe09.png)\n\n \n\nBayes' theorem states that\n\n$$\nP(A_1|B)=\\dfrac{P(B|A_1)P(A_1)}{P(B)}.\n$$\n\nNow, the law of total probability tells us that\n\n$$\nP(B)=P(B|A_1)P(A_1) + P(B|A_2)P(A_2) + P(B|A_3)P(A_3).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Bayes' theorem, we get\n\n$$\nP(A_1 | B) = \\dfrac{P(B|A_1) P(A_1)}{P(B|A_1)P(A_1) + P(B|A_2)P(A_2) + P(B|A_3)P(A_3)} .\n$$\n\nUsing similar arguments, we can say that for $A_k$ for $k=1,2,3,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A_k | B) = \\dfrac{P(B|A_k) P(A_k)}{P(B|A_1)P(A_1) + P(B|A_2)P(A_2) + P(B|A_3)P(A_3)}\n$$\n\nwhich is usually written more compactly as\n\n$$\nP(A_k | B) = \\dfrac{P(B|A_k) P(A_k)}{\\sum_{i=1}^3 P(B|A_i)P(A_i)}.\n$$\n\nIt's easy to see how this generalizes. If the disjoint union $A_1 \\sqcup A_2 \\sqcup \\ldots \\sqcup A_n$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP(A_k | B) = \\dfrac{P(B|A_k) P(A_k)}{\\sum_{i=1}^n P(B|A_i)P(A_i)}\n$$\n\nLet's see an example of how to apply this theorem." :tutorial/id #uuid "bf17685f-9890-54cc-b8a2-15815558e87a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6969 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b6bd91e8-6598-5c56-9fa4-bf1144fc830d" :step/math-academy-id 42168 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167713}}] :activity/title "Extending Bayes' Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167047}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494026} :activity/id #uuid "cebad3dd-e992-52ed-b284-8c84be80cca2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048270 :topic/id #uuid "8e43115f-4416-5ec0-b698-3e53a959567b" :topic/math-academy-id 3286} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493998 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167118 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88acdedb-86f7-5376-9663-ed92e647a982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.174" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163787} {:answer/id #uuid "b8298e93-12cb-5948-9b0c-4d75b7d3a684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.199" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163788} {:answer/id #uuid "cca46cae-3f7b-5c75-a82f-2a44e0f21afb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.203" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163789} {:answer/id #uuid "1bae2866-554f-511e-86e4-7f2ca81b63aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163790} {:answer/id #uuid "7352704f-f734-5a12-b655-8b2ef85bf0d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.191" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163791}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163788} :answer-field/id #uuid "42a0e7e2-d14d-5d36-a9fb-b4d391b04b02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dea495bf-07d9-54cd-baea-bb953539d3dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-148673" :question/problem "Arthur and Sara are finding the best recipe for chocolate muffins, and they test three different oven cooking modes, mode $1,$ mode $2,$ and mode $3.$ They also try two types of yeast, A and B.\n\n- They use mode $1$ to cook the $30%$ of their muffins, and $90%$ of those muffins turn out well.\n\n- They use mode $2$ to cook the $40%$ of their muffins, and $85%$ of those muffins turn out well.\n\n- They use mode $3$ to cook the $30%$ of their muffins, and $88%$ of those muffins turn out well.\n\nIf $20%$ of all muffins was prepared with yeast A, and $87%$ of yeast A muffins turn out well, what is the probability that a randomly chosen good muffin is done with yeast A? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the event that a muffin is made with yeast A, $G$ be the event that a muffin turns out well, and ${M}_{1},{M}_{2},{M}_{3}$ be the event that a muffin is cooked with mode $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({M}_{1})=0.3 &  & P(G|{M}_{1})=0.9 \\\\  & P({M}_{2})=0.4 &  & P(G|{M}_{2})=0.85 \\\\  & P({M}_{3})=0.3 &  & P(G|{M}_{3})=0.88\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${M}_{1}⊔{M}_{2}⊔{M}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({M}_{1})+P({M}_{2})+P({M}_{3})=0.3+0.4+0.3=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $20%$ of muffins are made with yeast A, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.2.\n$$\n\n- Since $87%$ of muffins made with yeast A turn out well, we have\n\n$$\nP(G|A)=0.87.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen good muffin is made with yeast A, which is represented as $P(A|G).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A|G) & =\\frac{P(G|A)P(A)}{P(G)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(G|A)P(A)}{P(G|{M}_{1})P({M}_{1})+P(G|{M}_{2})P({M}_{2})+P(G|{M}_{3})P({M}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.87)(0.2)}{(0.9)(0.3)+(0.85)(0.4)+(0.88)(0.3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.174}{0.874} \\\\  & ≈0.199,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "444842eb-2ff5-586c-acce-f30990803c2c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494071}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494026 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167117 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c5c4998-4883-5ba4-a08f-5c9044a9ad93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.584" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163781} {:answer/id #uuid "a9eec898-008f-5aeb-9d82-da5f4e5795a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.642" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163782} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f06ee9-62a4-5d90-a2a6-5b8a41e3e676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.527" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163783} {:answer/id #uuid "088444df-24a0-506a-90eb-e82abac77d6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.621" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163784} {:answer/id #uuid "4988a61c-c2b9-57dd-87f0-60ed10f9fa86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.592" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163785}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163785} :answer-field/id #uuid "4141da06-c478-52fb-832c-70aa2c84d531" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e39e7e2c-b39e-5302-b92e-8f13f6ffd98c" :question/math-academy-id "q-147235" :question/problem "The students in a particular school are split among three classes, class $1,$ class $2,$ and class $3.$\n\n- Class $1$ contains $25%$ of the students in the school, and $55%$ of those students take biology.\n\n- Class $2$ contains $35%$ of the students in the school, and $40%$ of those students take biology.\n\n- Class $3$ contains $40%$ of the students in the school.\n\nIf $45%$ of students in the entire school are female, $80%$ of female students take biology, and $70%$ of biology students are female, what is the probability that a randomly selected student from class $3$ takes biology? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $F$ be the event that a student is female, $B$ be the event that a student takes biology, and ${C}_{1},{C}_{2},{C}_{3}$ be the event that a student is in class $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({C}_{1})=0.25 &  & P(B|{C}_{1})=0.55 \\\\  & P({C}_{2})=0.35 &  & P(B|{C}_{2})=0.4 \\\\  & P({C}_{3})=0.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${C}_{1}⊔{C}_{2}⊔{C}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({C}_{1})+P({C}_{2})+P({C}_{3})=0.25+0.35+0.4=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $45%$ of students in the entire school are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.45.\n$$\n\n- Since $80%$ of females take biology, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|F)=0.8.\n$$\n\n- Since $70%$ of biology students are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F|B)=0.7.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen student from class $3$ takes biology, which is represented as $P(B|{C}_{3}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|B) & =\\frac{P(B|F)P(F)}{P(B)} \\\\ P(F|B) & =\\frac{P(B|F)P(F)}{P(B|{C}_{1})P({C}_{1})+P(B|{C}_{2})P({C}_{2})+P(B|{C}_{3})P({C}_{3})} \\\\ 0.7 & =\\frac{(0.8)(0.45)}{(0.55)(0.25)+(0.4)(0.35)+P(B|{C}_{3})(0.4)} \\\\ 0.7 & =\\frac{0.36}{0.2775+0.4P(B|{C}_{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(B|{C}_{3}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.7(0.2775+0.4P(B|{C}_{3})) & =0.36 \\\\ 0.2775+0.4P(B|{C}_{3}) & =\\frac{0.36}{0.7} \\\\ 0.2775+0.4P(B|{C}_{3}) & =\\frac{18}{35} \\\\ 0.4P(B|{C}_{3}) & =\\frac{18}{35}-0.2775 \\\\ P(B|{C}_{3}) & =\\frac{1}{0.4}(\\frac{18}{35}-0.2775) \\\\ P(B|{C}_{3}) & ≈0.592\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "84fa05d5-af97-59d8-b33c-dd583737eabb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494060}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494049 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493903 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0efdb1e8-9c52-52d4-b930-051a0d37a2ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493415} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd36a06-2c0d-5867-b8fb-b56a3f2b9dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.369" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493416} {:answer/id #uuid "9664ae4b-b407-5bfc-92af-7d685fbf9717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.431" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493626} {:answer/id #uuid "bd528e4f-8665-5726-9e95-265f3f2fbb42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.235" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493702} {:answer/id #uuid "e4fb6fc7-b587-569b-b74c-57c52fc7da96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493756}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493626} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae7e24b9-313e-560b-9e0c-e4316aa55761" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2ad5265b-a872-53d0-b3f1-65585e111dd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-149494" :question/problem "In a nature park, there are $4$ species of squirrels, each with the same population.\n\n- Among the squirrels of species $1,$ $12%$ are born in captivity.\n\n- Among the squirrels of species $2,$ $5%$ are born in captivity.\n\n- Among the squirrels of species $3,$ $6%$ are born in captivity.\n\n- Among the squirrels of species $4,$ $3%$ are born in captivity.\n\nIf $35%$ of the squirrels are adult females, and $8%$ of the adult female squirrels are born in captivity, what is the probability that a randomly chosen squirrel born in captivity is an adult female? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $F$ be the event that a squirrel is an adult female, $C$ be the event that a squirrel is born in captivity, and ${S}_{1},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},{S}_{4}$ be the event that a squirrel is of species $1,$ $2,$ $3,$ or $4,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- Each species has the same number of squirrels, so we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({S}_{1})=P({S}_{2})=P({S}_{3})=P({S}_{4})=0.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(C|{S}_{1})=0.12 \\\\  & P(C|{S}_{2})=0.05 \\\\  & P(C|{S}_{3})=0.06 \\\\  & P(C|{S}_{4})=0.03\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${S}_{1}⊔{S}_{2}⊔{S}_{3}⊔{S}_{4}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({S}_{1})+P({S}_{2})+P({S}_{3})+P({S}_{4})=0.25+0.25+0.25+0.25=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $35%$ of the squirrels are adult females, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.35.\n$$\n\n- Since $8%$ of adult females are born in captivity, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|F)=0.08.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen squirrel born in captivity is an adult female, which is represented as $P(F|C).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|C) & =\\frac{P(C|F)P(F)}{P(C)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(C|F)P(F)}{P(C|{S}_{1})P({S}_{1})+P(C|{S}_{2})P({S}_{2})+P(C|{S}_{3})P({S}_{3})+P(C|{S}_{3})P({S}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.08)(0.35)}{(0.12)(0.25)+(0.05)(0.25)+(0.06)(0.25)+(0.03)(0.25)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.028}{0.065} \\\\  & ≈0.431,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "b32c5a3a-fa6d-50eb-94d9-f684a00e861d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494060 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493912 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08123d50-e1d2-5e75-87c8-dc4ecec898fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493402} {:answer/id #uuid "2315c5ef-ec06-536c-af7c-d82e621541c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493448} {:answer/id #uuid "68e491ab-da9f-5329-9aaa-a11a1bebb3d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.047" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493547} {:answer/id #uuid "75ac382e-a141-5c83-ade6-7dc3a4ffc925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.035" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493566} {:answer/id #uuid "e67f42bf-f74e-5283-955d-76c3d0573b19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.040" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493760}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493760} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3f4b001-3f5d-563e-a82a-efaf92b09aad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "39f657eb-aa97-5394-b0e6-ecba3bf80018" :question/math-academy-id "q-148666" :question/problem "A manufacturing company has three sites, factory $1,$ factory $2,$ and factory $3.$ They all produce two types of light bulbs: A and B.\n\n- Factory $1$ produces $45%$ of the entire production, and $5%$ of those bulbs are defective.\n\n- Factory $2$ produces $45%$ of the entire production.\n\n- Factory $3$ produces $10%$ of the entire production, and $1%$ of those bulbs are defective.\n\nIf $57%$ of all light bulbs are of type A, $3%$ of type A light bulbs are defective, and $41%$ of defective light bulbs are of type A, what is the probability that a randomly chosen light bulb from factory $2$ is defective? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the event that a light bulb is of type A, $D$ be the event that a light bulb is defective, and ${F}_{1},{F}_{2},{F}_{3}$ be the event that a light bulb is produced in factory $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({F}_{1})=0.45 &  & P(D|{F}_{1})=0.05 \\\\  & P({F}_{2})=0.45 \\\\  & P({F}_{3})=0.1 &  & P(D|{F}_{3})=0.01\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${F}_{1}⊔{F}_{2}⊔{F}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({F}_{1})+P({F}_{2})+P({F}_{3})=0.45+0.45+0.1=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $57%$ of all light bulbs are of type A, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.57.\n$$\n\n- Since $3%$ of type A light bulbs are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.03.\n$$\n\n- Since $41%$ of defective light bulbs are of type A, we have\n\n$$\nP(A|D)=0.41\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen light bulb from factory $2$ is defective, which is represented as $P(D|{F}_{2}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A|D) & =\\frac{P(D|A)P(A)}{P(D)} \\\\ P(A|D) & =\\frac{P(D|A)P(A)}{P(D|{F}_{1})P({F}_{1})+P(D|{F}_{2})P({F}_{2})+P(D|{F}_{3})P({F}_{3})} \\\\ 0.41 & =\\frac{(0.03)(0.57)}{(0.05)(0.45)+P(D|{F}_{2})(0.45)+(0.01)(0.1)} \\\\ 0.41 & =\\frac{0.0171}{0.0235+0.45P(D|{F}_{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(D|{F}_{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.41(0.0235+0.45P(D|{F}_{2})) & =0.0171 \\\\ 0.0235+0.45P(D|{F}_{2}) & =\\frac{0.0171}{0.41} \\\\ 0.0235+0.45P(D|{F}_{2}) & =\\frac{171}{4100} \\\\ 0.45P(D|{F}_{2}) & =\\frac{171}{4100}-0.0235 \\\\ P(D|{F}_{2}) & =\\frac{1}{0.45}(\\frac{171}{4100}-0.0235) \\\\ P(D|{F}_{2}) & ≈0.040\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "e03d6f33-2586-5d57-a7b2-26ad674790fd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493998}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494071 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493967 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aad9a2ec-8c38-50c5-a148-e148cf1a201f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.307" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493659} {:answer/id #uuid "cb08ac4c-bcdb-5bf3-803d-48abd3c44b23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.546" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493717} {:answer/id #uuid "db3514aa-7da2-58cd-a765-4883b4c73d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493736} {:answer/id #uuid "e44bfd71-59f5-5456-8e29-b331fc4b994f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.398" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493751} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ca70ca-440e-5d09-bd91-1a12c84d2bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.252" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493758}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493751} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2b5eda7-fc54-5354-9c43-01751b2546c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "deb0627a-b1f4-5ecd-95e2-372ffa642f2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-149493" :question/problem "A volleyball school for children splits the players into three teams, team $1$, team $2,$ and team $3.$ \n\n- $44%$ of children are in team $1,$ and $23%$ of children in this team have been playing for over two years.\n\n- $32%$ of children are in team $2,$ and $26%$ of children in this team have been playing for over two years.\n\n- $24%$ of children are in team $3.$\n\nIf $7%$ of all children can play in the blocker position, $40%$ of children who can play as blockers have been playing for over two years, and $10%$ of children who have been playing for over two years can play as blockers, what is the probability that a randomly chosen child in team $3$ has been playing for over two years? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $B$ be the event that a child can play in the blocker position, $I$ be the event that a child has been playing for over two years, and ${T}_{1},{T}_{2},{T}_{3}$ be the events that a child is in team $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({T}_{1})=0.44 &  & P(I|{T}_{1})=0.23 \\\\  & P({T}_{2})=0.32 &  & P(I|{T}_{2})=0.26 \\\\  & P({T}_{3})=0.24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${T}_{1}⊔{T}_{2}⊔{T}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({T}_{1})+P({T}_{2})+P({T}_{3})=0.44+0.32+0.24=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $7%$ of children can play in the blocker position, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=0.07.\n$$\n\n- Since $40%$ of children who can play as blockers have been playing for over two years, we have\n\n$$\nP(I|B)=0.4.\n$$\n\n- Since $10%$ of children who have been playing for over two years can play as blockers, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|I)=0.1.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen child in team 3 has been playing for over two years, which is represented as $P(I|{T}_{3}).$\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B|I) & =\\frac{P(I|B)P(B)}{P(I)} \\\\ P(B|I) & =\\frac{P(I|B)P(B)}{P(I|{T}_{1})P({T}_{1})+P(I|{T}_{2})P({T}_{2})+P(I|{T}_{3})P({T}_{3})} \\\\ 0.1 & =\\frac{(0.4)(0.07)}{(0.23)(0.44)+(0.26)(0.32)+P(I|{T}_{3})(0.24)} \\\\ 0.1 & =\\frac{0.028}{0.1844+0.24P(I|{T}_{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(I|{T}_{3}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.1(0.1844+0.24P(I|{T}_{3})) & =0.028 \\\\ 0.1844+0.24P(I|{T}_{3}) & =\\frac{0.028}{0.1} \\\\ 0.1844+0.24P(I|{T}_{3}) & =\\frac{7}{25} \\\\ 0.24P(I|{T}_{3}) & =\\frac{7}{25}-0.1844 \\\\ P(I|{T}_{3}) & =\\frac{1}{0.24}(\\frac{7}{25}-0.1844) \\\\ P(I|{T}_{3}) & ≈0.398\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "f74c9cc2-11d5-584d-b43f-9184a7e00330" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494049}}] :activity/title "Extending Bayes' Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493388}]}
